// Package fr registers the French phonetic encoder, a consonant skeleton in the style of Double Metaphone with French spelling rules.
//
// The rules are written from standard French grapheme-to-phoneme correspondences (silent final consonants and plural marks, silent h, soft c and g before e/i/y, ch, ph, gn, qu, -tion, nasal m before b/p, the silent g of "doigt"), not ported from any implementation. Vowels are dropped after the first sound, as in Double Metaphone, which already merges the spellings of French vowels (é/er/ez/ai, o/au/eau, an/en). Voicing is merged as in Double Metaphone (b/p, d/t, v/f, s/z, hard g/k). A w after c, g, k or q is the glide of "oi" or "oua" as a recogniser may spell it for Québec French, where "quoi" is said [kwɛ] ("kwè"), as Ostiguy and Tousignant's Les prononciations du français québécois (2008) describes.
//
// Keys returns the key without the silent ending first and the key with its final consonant sounded second, so proper names that keep their final consonant still meet. Neither key sounds a plural s, x or z, and a consonant followed by "ed" keeps its d, since that is an English past participle in a French title ("paused", "imported").
package fr
